#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""
Run VS Code's Electron binary inside pasta and Bubblewrap on Fedora with Sway.

pasta owns the network namespace and Bubblewrap shares it, adding its own user, mount
and PID namespaces, a seccomp filter and a filesystem view limited to system trees,
toolchains, the VS Code profile and the project roots. The display is a Sway
security-context Wayland socket.

The launcher runs preflight, takes the single-instance lock in a host-only state
directory under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, writes the seccomp program, creates the
security-context listener, audits its globals and truncates code.log in the private
store, then forks the supervisor. The supervisor leaves the session, points its own
stdio at code.log and forks a guard, which runs

    pasta <net args> -- sh -c OUTER_STAGE <code.log> <seccomp> <fifo>
        bwrap <args> --seccomp 3 -- sh -c INNER_STAGE <command>

pasta's stdio is /dev/null: on Fedora it runs in SELinux's pasta_t, which may not
write code.log, so its own output is discarded. pasta closes every inherited
descriptor above 2, so the outer stage, back in the user's domain, reopens stdout and
stderr on code.log in append mode, the seccomp program on fd 3, which bwrap loads and
closes, and the ready FIFO's write end on fd 4. The inner stage runs after bwrap's
setup, seccomp included, writes one byte to fd 4 and closes it, so the command starts
with only stdin (/dev/null) and stdout and stderr (code.log). Once the byte arrives
the supervisor tells the launcher, which exits 0; the guard exiting first, or
READY_TIMEOUT passing, makes it exit 1 instead. A launcher that exits before then,
e.g. on Ctrl-C, makes the supervisor stop the sandbox.

pasta_t may not signal the sandbox, so neither pasta nor the parent-death signals of
its children stop bwrap or VS Code, and the exec into pasta_t clears pasta's own
parent-death signal. The guard, never exec'd and still in the user's domain, owns
teardown. It keeps only its stdio and the lock, and finds the outer stage (after its
exec the outer bwrap, PID 1 of pasta's PID namespace) from the host side as pasta's
namespace-init child. To stop the sandbox it SIGKILLs the outer bwrap through a
pidfd, which ends that namespace, or pasta while the outer stage is not found yet. As
a child subreaper it then kills and reaps whatever pasta leaves behind, so the lock
keeps a new launch out until nothing of the sandbox runs. It stops the sandbox when
pasta exits, when the supervisor dies (its parent-death signal), and when the
supervisor forwards SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP to it or gives up on readiness. It exits
with pasta's status.

The supervisor is a child subreaper as well, so a guard that dies first, killed or
crashed, leaves pasta and then the outer stage to the supervisor. After every guard
exit, before or after readiness, the supervisor reaps the guard, then kills and reaps
its own children until none remain. Only then does it close the close_fd write end,
revoking the Wayland listener, and remove the per-launch files, and the kernel
releases the lock. The death of both the supervisor and the guard leaves the sandbox
running without the lock.
"""

